.TH LPUI 1 "2026-11-13" "lazyplan-tui 0.44.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lpui \- local plan journal for you and your coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lpui
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR [\fICOMMAND OPTIONS\fR]]
.br
.B lazyplan
is the same program under its long name.
.br
With no COMMAND, \fBlpui\fR opens the keyboard\-driven screen (same as \fBlpui ui\fR).
.SH DESCRIPTION
lazyplan keeps your plans, milestones, and tasks in one SQLite database: shared in your home by default, or inside a repo after \fBlpui setup \-\-placement repo\fR. A plan is a title, a reason why it exists, a description of what to build, and a checklist that says when it is done; you break the plan into milestones and each milestone into tasks. You drive the keyboard\-driven screen; your coding agent drives the same database through this command line with \fB\-\-actor NAME\fR and \fB\-\-json\fR. Every change adds an entry to the feed; the running screen refreshes and tells you what arrived.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-db PATH
which database file to use (default: $LAZYPLAN_DB, else \./\.agents/lazyplan/ in a repo, else the global one)
.TP
.B \-\-json
print output as JSON so scripts and agents can read it
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
print terse tab\-separated rows for active agents (\-\-json still wins)
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the installed version and exit
.TP
.B \-\-actor NAME
who is making this change (you see it in feed; default: your login name or 'human')
.SH COMMANDS
Flags work before or after the command: \fBlpui list \-\-json\fR means the same as \fBlpui \-\-json list\fR. Run \fBlpui COMMAND \-\-help\fR for details.
